  "title": "BCIT Traffic Complexity",
  "description": "EEG and behavioral recordings from participants performing an extended simulated driving task with modulated visual complexity and perturbation frequency. The study aimed to identify driver fatigue and task performance changes through EEG-based biomarkers, compared against objective performance measures and a non-fatigued calibration driving session. It extends the Baseline Driving paradigm by adding pedestrians and traffic and varying how often lateral perturbations occurred.",
  "methods_description": "Participants drove in a simulator (steering wheel and pedals) on a long straight road and corrected lateral perturbations to stay in the right-most lane. Recordings included 64-channel BioSemi EEG (plus 4 eye and 2 mastoid channels, 2048 Hz), SMI REDEYE250 eye tracking, and vehicle logs at 100 Hz. Task order versus Baseline Driving was counter-balanced. Fatigue and sleepiness questionnaires (TIFS, KSS, VAS-F) were collected.",
